# APEX session handoff

This project is edited by two Cursor instances on two machines
(day = Computer A, night = Computer B), both signed into the same account
and pushing to `main` on `adnanmoffice-bt/trader1`. Without discipline they
will stomp on each other's work. Follow this protocol **exactly**.

## On session start (do this before answering the user's first real request)

1. Run `git fetch origin` and `git status`. If `origin/main` is ahead of
   local, run `git pull --ff-only origin main` before touching any file.
2. Read `HANDOFF.md` end-to-end (root of repo).
3. Read `CONTEXT.md` (architecture + hard-won lessons) and the most recent
   10 commits with `git log --oneline -10`.
4. If `HANDOFF.md` has an active `LOCK:` line, stop and ask the user
   whether the other machine is still mid-edit before continuing.
5. If the user's request overlaps an item already listed under
   **OPEN WORK**, acknowledge that explicitly.

## On session end (do this when the user's task is complete or when they stop)

1. Update `HANDOFF.md`:
   - Prepend a new **SESSION LOG** entry at the top with date (Dubai
     time), machine (Computer A/day or Computer B/night), commit SHA,
     short context, and safety notes.
   - Add/tick items under **OPEN WORK**.
   - Remove any `LOCK:` lines you set earlier in the session.
2. `git add -A && git commit -m "<conventional message>" && git push origin main`.
3. Never leave uncommitted changes at end of session (except private env
   files or the `.git/COMMIT_MSG_TMP.txt` scratch file). The other machine
   must be able to `git pull` and be in the same state you left.

## Mid-session, if you're about to edit a high-risk file

High-risk files: `agents/war-room.ts`, `agents/meta-agent.ts`,
`lib/safety.ts`, `lib/risk-controls.ts`, `lib/exchanges/*`,
`app/api/cron/*`, `supabase/schema.sql`.

Before a multi-step refactor on any of these:
1. Add a `LOCK:` line to `HANDOFF.md` (e.g.
   `LOCK: agents/war-room.ts — Computer A — started 2026-04-24 10:00 Dubai`).
2. Push that `LOCK:` commit immediately so the other machine sees it.
3. Clear the lock (and push) when you're done.

## Commit hygiene

- One logical change per commit.
- Conventional-ish prefixes: `feat:`, `fix:`, `fix(safety):`, `ui:`,
  `i18n:`, `chore:`, `CRITICAL:` (user-facing breakage being fixed).
- Always push. Both machines are consumers of `origin/main`.
- On Windows PowerShell, heredoc doesn't work — write commit messages to
  `.git/COMMIT_MSG_TMP.txt` and commit with `git commit -F`.

## Never do

- Don't `git push --force` to `main`.
- Don't rebase `main` history.
- Don't commit `.env.local`, `.env*`, or anything under `.vercel/`.
- Don't amend a pushed commit unless the user explicitly says so.
- Don't re-enable: shorts, SOL/USD, BNB/USD, BB_SQUEEZE trigger. Document
  reasons for these in `CONTEXT.md` → "Tvrde, naučene istine".

## Things both machines should stay in sync on

- `CONTEXT.md` — architecture, invariants.
- `HANDOFF.md` — who did what, what's pending.
- `APEX_PROJECT_LOG.md` — longer-form changelog.
- `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` — behaviour rules.
- `scripts/*` — debug + audit scripts (commit them; don't leave them
  untracked forever).
